  "image_name": "1ce48736200c151ee8e2fe8faa944d1c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts ordinary single-variant analysis with haplotype-based analysis and shows why accurate phasing matters. At the top, two colored chromosome lines represent the two true haplotypes across several variant positions. The phased results illustrate two common mistakes: a switch error, where phase assignment changes between nearby sites, and a flip error, where the two chromosome labels are reversed but the haplotype structure is otherwise preserved.\n\nThe lower half compares how genetic association can be interpreted in a population. On the left, “variant association” groups people by genotype at individual sites (for example G/G, G/C, C/C), with cases highlighted in blue. The corresponding quantitative trait distributions differ across these genotype classes, showing a standard single-marker effect.\n\nOn the right, “haplotype association” focuses instead on a particular multi-site chromosome pattern, marked in red, versus other haplotypes in gray. Cases are enriched among individuals carrying the highlighted haplotype, and the trait distributions shift according to haplotype status rather than any one variant alone. Overall, the figure emphasizes that phased haplotypes capture joint genetic effects across linked variants, but interpretation depends on phasing accuracy.",
